<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>GEMS · Evidence &amp; submission review</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><main><p class="stop"><strong>ARCHIVED SNAPSHOT.</strong> <a href="index.html">See the current dashboard</a>.</p><article style="white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere"># Executive scientific review — 26 October 2026

## Decision: DO NOT SUBMIT

There is **no eligible TIFF, no new leaderboard estimate, and no competition upload in this recovery session**. The single method lane referenced in the parallel-run protocol was not included in the request. More importantly, an independently verified registry witness makes the inherited literal uniqueness check impossible to pass for any nonempty candidate within the mirrored sample footprint. Stopping before further candidate placement is required; the recovery deliverable is negative, not a claim that the geology failed.

## Prior parallel work preserved

A concurrent provisional bilateral K/Th–LiDAR experiment reported **HOLDOUT-DTI 0.1133855**, 95% CI **[0.1006053, 0.1262007]**, `gems57-pooled-hide-v2`, 60,988 withheld positives. Its paired delta against its same-fold feature ablation was **+0.0009773**, 95% CI **[−0.0005962, +0.0026773]**; the comparable best-incumbent gate remains untested. Its 37,654 in-memory dots had 100% overlap with a registered lattice; that lane did not write a TIFF. See its [record](../evidence/run-card.json) and the [preserved prior review](../archive/recovery/concurrent-main-research-review.md).

A separate GPC-3 research TIFF already on main had 37,654 of 37,654 dots within 3 pixels of that lattice ([independent audit](../evidence/parallel-lattice-audit.json)). The other session reported **HOLDOUT-DTI 0.1376815**, 95% CI **[0.1255670, 0.1503305]**, `gems-segment-pooled-v1`, 60,988 positives; we did not rerun it, and the evaluator is not demonstrated interchangeable with the bilateral evaluator. The TIFF is research-only and **not an eligible submission**. These historical experiments are distinct from this session&#x27;s data recovery; neither used a competition slot or supplies an organizer receipt.

## What was recovered and measured

1. The shared <a href="https://github.com/buffedlizard55-lab/57GEMSDOE/tree/822a0ce5546c7b6749cdd82eb145b6b3e616603d">57GEMSDOE template</a> evaluator, writer, metric, holdout and related code were recovered **unmodified** and checked against [pins](../registry/shared_template_pins.json). They live in ignored `.cache/`; their license for reuse has not been established. They are participant code, not an organizer evaluator. Several sibling repositories identify different templates, so this pin is a reproducible candidate template—not proof of the missing lane&#x27;s intended template.
2. A public third-party bridge was independently reassembled from five SHA-checked parts. The shared template&#x27;s original `cache_bands` function produced 19 cached bands, each matching the corresponding template hash. A second clean-directory bridge recovery was also verified. [Recovery receipt](../evidence/feature_cache_recovery_20261026.json); reproduce with `python scripts/restore_shared_template.py --cache-features`. Large rasters/caches are ignored. Hashes establish mirror consistency, not organizer origin, license, correctness of band descriptions or absence of leakage.
3. The independently recovered participant-mirror sample has 60,988 positive cells, all exactly matching the mirrored labels, although the <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/967/#submission-format">organizer describes its sample</a> as predicting total fault absence. This is a provenance irregularity, not evidence that the organizer&#x27;s actual sample has those values. Use this mirror only for grid/footprint checks pending an authenticated sample and license verification.
4. A pinned <a href="https://github.com/buffedlizard55-lab/GEMSDOE31/blob/2a7cd0c8f1c30341a3a655a01a1ff5bff67b23d2/registry/raster_registry.json">GEMSDOE31 raster registry</a> claims 716 distinct dot sets from 92 repositories as of 2026-10-25. We restored its manifest, **not all 716 raster bytes or a current complete registry**. It contains an <a href="https://github.com/buffedlizard55-lab/GEMSDOE48/blob/268ec599f51c517cc3485da077c24d83af5be81d/docs/downloads/GEMSDOE48-unassigned-mass-20261006.tif">unassigned-mass diagnostic</a>. Its hash, CRS, shape and transform were checked against the pinned mirror; the unchanged shared `saturation_certificate` found its positive support covers all 5,167,373 footprint pixels. Thus, **if every positive cell of every indexed registry raster is treated as a dot**, any nonempty candidate within that footprint has 100% directed overlap with this raster—above the 70% stop. [Reproducible geometric certificate](../evidence/registry_saturation_20261026.json).
5. This witness is a **continuous diagnostic**, not an established prior dot submission. The protocol says compare registry rasters&#x27; dots but does not define whether diagnostics count; the shared implementation defines dots as finite values greater than zero. Do not silently exclude it, claim the run is unique, or substitute a density exemption. Resolve the registry scope/representation in the common protocol before a scientific run. The certificate is conditional on the mirrored footprint and the referenced registry scope, not a proof about an unobserved official registry.
6. For forensic learning only, the nine historical winners were restored from pinned participant repositories, hash-verified, and compared independently. All 72 directed winner-to-winner 3-pixel comparisons exceed the requested 70% stop; the minimum is 86.718954%. Continuous-surface correlations have not been measured. Each historical file is therefore a duplicate **under this protocol**, not an eligible new submission. [Audit](../docs/audit.json).

## Why H33 B2 might have ranked higher

The supplied GEMSDOE32 value **0.2778 is an unverified user/owner report**, not HOLDOUT-DTI or an organizer receipt binding exact bytes. Its sparse H19-5 descendant and near-catalogue thinning are plausible contributors: if non-catalogue pixels removed by thinning have little hidden-fault credit, their removal can lower weighted false-positive mass; too much thinning can also reduce coverage/recall. This is a hypothesis, not an ablation result.

A <a href="https://community.drivendata.org/t/scoring-clarification-are-known-usgs-ingenious-faults-masked-when-scoring-and-are-they-in-the-final-round-label-set/11516/4">DrivenData staff clarification</a> says the known-fault mask is **pixel-exact**, identical to the provided training labels, and used in both rounds. Removing predictions exactly on those pixels does not itself lower the penalty. Nearby unmasked predictions far from new-fault truth are penalized; new-fault truth can also be within 300 m of a known trace. Thus the effect of near-fault thinning depends on hidden truth. Neither private labels nor a score-to-hash receipt are available, so there is no defensible basis to promise an improvement over the reported value.

## Metric irregularity found during review

The official <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/967/#performance-metric">DTI definitions</a> sum true-positive credit over **truth** cells and false-positive contributions over **prediction** cells. Their weighted totals need not coincide. Therefore the simplification `denominator = alpha * predicted_mass + beta * truth_count` is not generally valid. A checked synthetic case shows one prediction can add credit to several truth cells, so even the prose inequality `delta TP &lt;= one prediction&#x27;s nearest-truth credit` is not general. The pinned `gems57.metric.dti_exact` agreed with its brute-force implementation on that test; the questionable statement is prose, and the older GEMSDOE32 explanation also uses an unjustified simplification. No shared source was privately patched. Correct this prose once in the upstream shared template before using it to justify emission rules; local regression test: `test_shared_metric_synthetic_counterexample_when_available`.

## Format, range error, and false certainty

The official description requires one `float32` band in EPSG:32611, 100 m resolution, matching bounds, values in [0,1], and null/NaN outside the bounds. It does **not** establish exactly how the portal interprets the mirrored sample&#x27;s irregular mask. The local validator applies a conservative sample-mask policy; 7 of the 9 historical rasters fail that **local policy** because they use finite zeros outside that mask, while 2 pass. This does not prove those 7 were or would be rejected by DrivenData. The earlier report incorrectly called this an established official-format failure; that claim is withdrawn.

The reported portal error cannot be diagnosed without the exact uploaded TIFF. The revised validator rejects empty/ambiguous footprints, infinity, invalid in-footprint values, mismatched geometry, and external `.msk` sidecar dependence; it reports whether all raw bytes are finite and in range. A synthetic internal-mask test demonstrates that a standalone TIFF can contain finite zeros outside while marking them invalid in its internal mask, but organizer acceptance and compatibility with the shared writer have not been established. Do not silently clip, repair, or resubmit an old file.

## Verified official context and limits

- <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/967/">Problem and DTI</a>: the target is expert-identified geological **faults**, not proof of a geothermal vent. The same selected submission is used for private initial scoring and the expanded final-label round.
- <a href="https://community.drivendata.org/t/scoring-clarification-are-known-usgs-ingenious-faults-masked-when-scoring-and-are-they-in-the-final-round-label-set/11516/4">Organizer clarification on known-fault masks</a>: the mask is pixel-exact, not a 300 m buffer. Nearby unmasked predictions can be penalized and new-fault truth can occur within 300 m of known faults.
- <a href="https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/96647.pdf">NLR official rules, sections 3.2–3.5</a>: the PDF describes three submissions per week, selection of one final submission for both rounds, reproducible code/documentation and generative-AI disclosure. The authenticated team&#x27;s remaining quota and current form were not checked. The <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/">competition overview</a> gives a deadline of 3 December 2026 at 23:59 UTC; recheck the live site.
- <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/leaderboard/">Official public leaderboard</a> was fetched on 2026-10-26; it is not a receipt for any of these site submissions. Use the link for current standings; no candidate leaderboard estimate or organizer-confirmed team score is inferred from it.
- <a href="https://www.usgs.gov/data/geodawn-airborne-magnetic-and-radiometric-surveys-northwestern-great-basin-nevada-and">USGS GeoDAWN</a> identifies magnetic, radiometric and GeoTIFF products and a CC0 designation. <a href="https://gdr.openei.org/submissions/1391">GDR INGENIOUS #1391</a> lists a publicly accessible CC BY 4.0 compilation including 2 m probes and well/spring chemistry. These official metadata pages were read; underlying external downloads, coverage and competition-use compatibility were **not** independently tested.
- The <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/data/">official data tab</a> redirected to login. No organizer-authenticated data or account receipt is available. Do not store credentials. Review <a href="https://www.drivendata.org/competitions/306/competition-doe-gems/page/967/#external-datasets">external data rules</a> before using any mirror or derived feature.

## Exact continuation

1. Define the missing single-method lane and settle whether dense diagnostics belong in the dot registry, centrally; do not weaken thresholds in this project alone.
2. Verify permission/license for the shared template and bridge and compare their bytes against an authorized organizer download. Resolve the sample discrepancy and competing template pins.
3. Restore and freeze the current complete surface/dot registry and immutable inventory; a stale manifest or absent bytes cannot establish uniqueness.
4. Only then preregister the lane&#x27;s top hypothesis. Rebuild all catalogue-derived features from visible faults in buffered whole-segment folds, run every feature-alone AUC canary, and compare candidate versus the current incumbent using the same pooled shared HOLDOUT-DTI evaluator and paired spatial-block 95% CI. Maximum three experiments or two hours; stop on a duplicate.
5. If scientific gates clear, use the shared writer and independently validate exact saved bytes. A separate selector checks the authenticated current quota and decides a slot. Preserve the organizer receipt, exact hash, name, and note. No user/owner report or projection is an organizer-confirmed score.
